t this conjecture is false by providing a constructive method to build three infinite graph families with a defective eigenvalue (see Section 4).\nElectronic Journal of Linear Algebra, ISSN 1081-3810  \nA publication of the International Linear Algebra Society  \nVolume 41, pp. 511-528, September 2025.\n\nDEFECTIVE EIGENVALUES OF THE NON-BACKTRACKING MATRIX*\n\nKRISTIN HEYSSEN†, KATE LORENZEN‡, AND CAROLYN REINHART§\n\n**Abstract.** We consider graphs for which the non-backtracking matrix has defective eigenvalues or graphs for which the matrix does not have a full set of eigenvectors. The existence of these values results in Jordan blocks of size greater than one, which are called nontrivial. We develop a relationship between the eigenspaces of the non-backtracking matrix and the eigenspaces of a smaller matrix, completely classifying their differences among graphs with at most one cycle. Finally, we provide several constructions of infinite graph families that have nontrivial Jordan blocks for both this smaller matrix and the non-backtracking matrix.\n\n**Key words.** Non-backtracking matrix, Jordan form, Non-backtracking walks on graphs.\n\n**AMS subject classifications.\nThe non-backtracking matrix is not symmetric, making it one of the only well-studied graph matrices where there may not be a full set of linearly independent eigenvectors. When the algebraic and geometric multiplicity of an eigenvalue are not equal, the eigenvalue is called *defective*, and the matrix is not diagonalizable. Graphs that have a defective eigenvalue we will call *defective graphs*. We look to answer the question, *which graphs are defective for the non-backtracking matrix?* If a graph has a vertex of degree one, then the non-backtracking matrix of the graph will have a defective eigenvalue of λ = 0. As such, the question can be rephrased: *which graphs of minimum degree two are defective for the non-backtracking matrix?* It was conjectured by Torres in [19] that the answer is never.\n\nWe show that this conjecture is false by providing a constructive method to build three infinite graph families with a defective eigenvalue (see Section 4). In some cases, this defective eigenvalue is real. In addition, we provide some numerical data regarding the number of graphs on ten or fewer vertices with\n\n*Received by the editors on July 16, 2024. Accepted for publication on September 10, 2025. Handling Editor: Vanni Noferini. Corresponding Author: Kristin Heysse.*  \n†Department of Mathematics, Statistics, and Computer Science, Macalester College, St. Paul, MN, USA (kheysse@macalester.edu).  \n‡Department of Mathematics and Computer Science, Linfield University, McMinnville, OR, USA (klorenzen@linfield.edu)  \n§Department of Mathematics and Statistics, Swarthmore College, Swarthmore, PA, USA (creinhal@swarthmore.edu)--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nFoatic Homomesy (https://arxiv.cnoctave.top/pdf/2610.40882)\nciteturn0search14 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 5 days ago; [ELM+24, Lemma 3.11] that for a given set \\(X\\), two statistics \\(s_1,s_2:X\\to\\mathbb C\\), and an action \\(\\tau:X\\to X\\), if \\(s_1\\) is \\(c_1\\)-mesic under \\(\\tau\\) and \\(s_2\\) is \\(c_2\\)-mesic under \\(\\tau\\), then linear combinations of \\(s_1\\) and \\(s_2\\) are also homomesic. ... ## 5.1 Proof of Theorem 3.1 ... ** To prove that fix is 1-mesic under the action of complement-inversion, complement-rotation, reversal-rotation, and reversal-inversion, by Lemma 4.1, it will be sufficient to prove that for all \\(\\pi\\in S_n\\) and \\(\\tau\\) as any of the aforementioned actions,\n**Proof.** Let such a closed walk alternating between the vertices visited and the edges taken be given by\n\\[\nx_1,\\tau_{i_1},x_2,\\tau_{i_2},\\ldots,\\tau_{i_l},x_{l+1}=x_1\n\\]\nwhere\n\\[\nl=\\sum_{j=1}^{m}a_j.\n\\]\nThen\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{1}{l}\\sum_{j=1}^{l}s(x_j)\n&=\\frac{1}{l}\\sum_{j=1}^{l}\\big(f(x_j)-f(\\tau_{i_j}(x_j