igenvalue.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nThe real eigenvalues of the non-backtracking Laplacian (https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.02194)\nciteturn0academia16 [wordlim: 200] Published: 6 days ago; Authors: Raffaella Mulas, Leo Torres ... We prove that the non-backtracking Laplacian of $G$ has at least $2M-2N+2c+2b$ real eigenvalues, counted with algebraic multiplicity, where $c$ is the number of connected components of $G$ and $b$ is the number of bipartite ones. ... We show that the bound $2M-2N+2c+2b$ is sharp, and that a connected $d$-regular graph attains it if and only if it is strictly Ramanujan, i.e., every adjacency eigenvalue $μ\\ne\\pm d$ satisfies $|μ|<2\\sqrt{d-1}$.\nTitle: The real eigenvalues of the non-backtracking Laplacian\nAuthors: Raffaella Mulas, Leo Torres\nDate: Sun Nov  1 20:45:33 2026\n\nLet $G$ be a simple graph on $N$ nodes and $M$ edges, with minimum degree at least $2$. We prove that the non-backtracking Laplacian of $G$ has at least $2M-2N+2c+2b$ real eigenvalues, counted with algebraic multiplicity, where $c$ is the number of connected components of $G$ and $b$ is the number of bipartite ones. More precisely, at least $M-N+2c$ of them lie in $[0,1)$ and at least $M-N+2b$ lie in $(1,2]$. We show that the bound $2M-2N+2c+2b$ is sharp, and that a connected $d$-regular graph attains it if and only if it is strictly Ramanujan, i.e., every adjacency eigenvalue $μ\\ne\\pm d$ satisfies $|μ|<2\\sqrt{d-1}$. For completeness, we also show that the non-backtracking Laplacian has at most $2M-2c-2b$ real eigenvalues.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nDefective eigenvalues of the non-backtracking matrix - arXiv Combinatorics (https://arxiv.symmetricfunctions.com/paper/2407.12106v1)\nciteturn0search1 [wordlim: 200] Published: 2.3 years ago; Crawled: last month; We consider graphs for which the non-backtracking matrix has defective eigenvalues, or graphs for which the matrix does not have a full set of eigenvectors. ... Finally, we provide several constructions of infinite graph families that have nontrivial Jordan blocks for both this smaller matrix and the non-backtracking matrix.\n\n## Defective eigenvalues of the non-backtracking matrix\n\nKristin Heysse, Kate Lorenzen, Carolyn Reinhart\n\nPublished: 2024-07-16\n\nNote:\n\nPublication:\n\ngraph\n\n# • BIB • PDF • ARXIV •\n\n### Abstract\n\nWe consider graphs for which the non-backtracking matrix has defective eigenvalues, or graphs for which the matrix does not have a full set of eigenvectors. The existence of these values results in Jordan blocks of size greater than one, which we call nontrivial. We show a relationship between the eigenspaces of the non-backtracking matrix and the eigenspaces of a smaller matrix, completely classifying their differences among graphs with at most one cycle. Finally, we provide several constructions of infinite graph families that have nontrivial Jordan blocks for both this smaller matrix and the non-backtracking matrix.\n\n### BibTeX\n\n[Button: arXiv BibTeX ] [Button: Published BibTeX ]\n\nMaintained by Per Alexandersson, per@symmetricfunctions.com--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nThe Perron non-backtracking eigenvalue after node addition (https://arxiv.org/abs/2111.09037)\nciteturn0academia17 [wordlim: 200] Published: 5.0 years ago; Authors: Leo Torres ... Consider a finite undirected unweighted graph G and add a new node to it arbitrarily connecting it to pre-existing nodes. We study the behavior of the Perron eigenvalue of the non-backtracking matrix of G before and after such a node addition. ... Our results depend on the assumption of diagonalizability of the non-backtracking matrix.\nTitle: The Perron non-backtracking eigenvalue after node addition\nAuthors: Leo Torres\nDate: Wed Nov 17 11:22:12 2021\n\nConsider a finite undirected unweighted graph G and add a new node to it arbitrarily connecting it to pre-existing nodes. We study the behavior of the Perron eigenvalue of the non-backtracking matrix of G before and after such a node addition. We prove an interlacing-type result for said eigenvalue, namely, the Perron eigenvalue never decreases after node addition. Furthermore, our methods lead to bounds for the difference between the eigenvalue before and after node addition. These are the first known bounds that have been established in full rigor. Our results depend on the assumption of diagonalizability of the non-backtracking matrix. Practical experience says that this assumption is fairly mild in many families of graphs, though necessary and sufficient conditions for it remain an open question.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nPotential counter-examples to a conjecture on the column space of the adjacency matrix (https://www.researchgate.net/publication/390666203_Potential_counter-examples_to_a_conjecture_on_the_column_space_of_the_adjacency_matrix)\nciteturn0search2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 months ago; Let G be a base graph with 0 as a non-main eigenvalue. ... Regular graphs lie in the set of graphs for which the conjecture holds. ... The criterion in Theorem 3.4 characterizes a counter-example (if any exists) to the ACK-conjecture for a\n\nv\n\nis a dominating vertex in +G\n\nv\n\n, then\n\nv\n\nis a\n\nCFV\n\nup\n\np\n\nin\n\n+G\n\nv\n\n. The following result is immediate from Theorem 4.5.\n\nCorollary 4.6. A potential counter-example is a base graph with 0 as a main eigenvalue.\n\nAdding a dominating vertex to a base graph G with 0 as a main eigenvalue, however does not violate the\n\nconditions for G to be a counter-example to the ACK-conjecture. Therefore, to con ﬁ rm that a core graph with 0\n\nas a main eigenvalue is a counter-example to the ACK-conjecture, all possible ways of adding a vertex other\n\nthan a pendant or a dominating vertex need to be checked.\n\nProposit